            <div xml:id="d158">

                <pb facs="0264" n="182" sameAs="frus:frus1925v02/pg182"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">862.51/2109: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Germany</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Schurman</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary
                        of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Berlin</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1925-11-14T13:00:00+01:00"><hi rendition="#italic">November 14,
                                1925—1 p.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received November 14—12:30
                        p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>196. Department’s telegram number 196, November 11, 6 p.m. Question 2: Acting
                    Chief, Advisory Board, stated yesterday that since public statement quoted in my
                    despatch 408 [<hi rendition="#italic">409</hi>]<anchor n="70" xml:id="d158-f1"/>
                    following loans have been approved by Board: Bremen, Düsseldorf, group of
                    Wurtemberg <pb facs="0265" n="183" xml:id="pg183"/> cities including Stuttgart,
                    Frankfort am Main, Nuremberg, State of Oldenburg; and definite approval imminent
                    for a few others now pending. Question 3: Board has jurisdiction as to foreign
                    financing by states as well as communes. Question 4: The Board does not have
                    jurisdiction over industrial loans.</p>
                <p rendition="#center">[Paraphrase]</p>
                <p>For reasons explained in my despatch 295, September 15, very effective control
                    cannot be exerted by Advisory Board over loans to municipalities. It is stated
                    by the Acting Chief of the Advisory Board that it would be necessary to amend
                    the constitution in order to make control by the board effective, as the various
                    states can at any time withdraw their consent to be represented on the board. It
                    is not my impression as matters now stand that the Advisory Board or any other
                    German agency will be in a position in the long run to control effectively the
                    obtaining of loans from abroad.</p>
                <p>Please inform Department of Commerce.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Schurman</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
